    updated: "2026-11-01",
    stackLine: "Astro · TypeScript · Content collections · Vitest · Playwright + axe",
    service: { href: "/services/travel-website-development/", label: "Travel & taxi website development" },
    title: "Sunny Himachal Travels — Tour & Taxi Website · Divyansh Sood",
    description: "A tour and taxi site for a Kangra valley operator: a page for every trip, transfer and town, 1.1 KB of its own JavaScript, and Lighthouse 100 ×4 on desktop.",
    ogTitle: "Sunny Himachal Travels — tours & taxi website · Case study",
    ogDescription: "A page for every trip, transfer and town. 1.1 KB of its own JavaScript. Lighthouse 100 ×4.",
    ogImage: "https://www.divyanshsood.com/images/og/sunny-himachal-travels.jpg",
    keywords: "travel agency website Himachal, taxi website developer, tour operator website Kangra, Dharamshala taxi website, Himachal tour packages website, Astro travel site, Google spam update travel site, WhatsApp enquiry form, Divyansh Sood",
    feature: { src: "/images/work/sunny-himachal-travels-1.webp", alt: "Sunny Himachal Travels — homepage hero with the WhatsApp trip-planning button" },
    body: [
      { tag: "The brief", title: "A good design that wasn't a website", type: "prose",
        html: `The canvas was finished and its copy was genuinely good: plain, specific, no brochure language. So it was carried across word for word. Underneath, though, every style was an inline string, interactivity ran through a <strong>69 KB runtime</strong>, and images were empty placeholders. The job was to turn that one page into a site that ranks for what travellers actually search: a trip, a route, a town.` },
      { tag: "Stack", type: "list", items: ["Astro, static output", "TypeScript (strict)", "Content collections + Zod", "Hand-written CSS tokens", "Vitest, Playwright + axe"] },
      { tag: "What we shipped", title: "One page per search", type: "prose",
        html: `There are <strong>25 tour routes</strong>, <strong>28 destinations</strong>, <strong>8 point-to-point transfers</strong>, <strong>9 local “taxi in &lt;town&gt;” pages</strong>, 7 planning guides and 15 blog posts: 106 routes in all, every one written as Markdown with typed frontmatter. The link graph is reciprocal: name a region on a trip and the trip appears on the region's page, with no one editing it. The enquiry form writes the WhatsApp message for the traveller, and a sticky bar keeps WhatsApp and Call one tap away on a phone.` },
      { tag: "Guardrails", title: "Nothing ships unchecked", type: "prose",
        html: `A page is a draft until it's marked <code>verified</code>. Drafts render at their URL for checking, but they're <code>noindex</code> and left out of the sitemap: eight of the nine town pages are verified today, and Tanda waits on the operator. Prices have a separate confirmation flag, which is still off for all 25 tours, so no tour price is shown. <code>npm run verify</code> fails if any of 23 colour pairs drops below WCAG AA, and the suite behind it is <strong>190 unit tests, 62 over the built site and 1,165 Playwright tests</strong> on desktop and mobile, with axe reporting zero violations.` },
      { tag: "Duplicate content", title: "A build that refuses thin pages", type: "prose",
        html: `Growing from one page to about a hundred is exactly where travel sites go thin. A build test compares every pair of pages on five-word shingles and <strong>fails above 0.23 similarity</strong>. The most similar pair among the 25 trips scores 0.077, and among the nine town pages 0.066. Routes that would have overlapped an existing page were dropped instead of being written thin.` },
      { tag: "Search Console", title: "The first five weeks, read honestly", type: "prose",
        html: `From the first real day of impressions (8 September) to 8 October the site drew <strong>8,695 impressions and 70 clicks at an average position of about 12.8</strong>. Phones did nearly all of the work: 64 of the 70 clicks, at position 8.5. The query the business cares about most moved the right way: <em>kangra taxi service</em> went from 9 impressions to 48 to 58 across three consecutive periods, from a position near 26 to about 15. Every figure comes from the raw Search Console exports kept in the repository, read period by period, not from a screenshot of the best day.` },
      { tag: "The spam update", title: "Two drops, neither of them ours", type: "prose",
        html: `Google ran a spam update from 24 September to 8 October. The site's distance posts took it: the Naddi and Dal Lake post fell from 215 impressions to 12 between two periods, Kangra Fort and Masroor from 92 to 8. None had been edited since 9 September, every one still returned 200 with a self-canonical, and Search Console showed <strong>105 pages indexed before, during and after</strong>. They weren't de-indexed; they stopped being shown. In the same days, destination and taxi searches grew. One thing the export couldn't separate: the home page lost clicks in the week a layout change shipped, the same week as the update, and the write-up says so rather than blaming Google.` },
      { tag: "What changed", title: "Six routes folded, first fares live", type: "prose",
        html: `Fourteen near-identical route pages that rank nowhere is the shape a spam update counts against a whole site. So the six that never touch the Kangra valley (Chandigarh to Shimla, Delhi to Manali, Manali to Kaza and three more) became plain lines on <code>/taxi</code>, and their URLs 301 there; the eight that end in or near the valley kept their pages. On 11 October the first fares went live, straight from the operator's own notebook for the Ertiga: Pathankot ₹5,000, Amritsar and Chandigarh ₹9,000 to Dharamshala, plus airport and station lines. The <code>TaxiService</code> schema carries an <code>AggregateOffer</code>, and a build test fails if its low, high and count don't match the table on the page.` },
      { tag: "Trust", title: "Only facts somebody gave", type: "prose",
        html: `The address now matches the Google Business Profile, near the new bus stand in Kangra, where the cars are kept; trips start from Kangra, Dharamshala and Palampur. The rating strip shows <strong>5.0 from 19 Google reviews</strong>, read on the day it was published, with three testimonials quoted exactly as written and a link out to the listing. The fields for “since” and “trips run” exist in the code and are deliberately empty: a half-filled trust file produces a shorter strip, never a guess. None of it becomes review markup, which Google treats as self-serving on a business's own site. Even the maker credit in the footer is kept out of the structured data.` },
      { tag: "Details", type: "list", items: ["A region map drawn from 2011 Census district boundaries, simplified from 1,707 points to 620: 4.6 KB", "Markdown image titles become real figcaptions through a small plugin", "llms.txt and Speakable markup built from the same constants as the pages", "1.1 KB of the site's own JavaScript, measured before GA4 was added; desktop LCP 0.4 s, CLS 0"] },
      { tag: "The lesson", type: "prose",
        html: `A travel site ranks one page at a time. Someone typing “taxi service in Palampur” wants the Palampur page, not the homepage. And it can lose one page at a time too, which is why every page has to earn its place. More on the page structure in <a href="/blog/taxi-tour-operator-website-pages/">what pages a taxi or tour operator website needs</a>.` },
    ],
